// Longform read: "Inflation: The Price of Nearly Everything" — WORKING DRAFT.
// Blueprint: the read's anatomy plan in the internal editorial docs.
// Parts I-III built. Part II restructured as the climb (Jul 2026), then
// reversed into the descent (Aug 2026): headline first → divisions/beam →
// funnel → the item that won't split → collectors → the two averages. The
// averaging collapsible (AM/GM fold + UK RPI story) is TODO — see the marker
// in Part II.
// Finalization pass (2026-08-01): the base-effect section ("How to read the
// chart") is PARKED, not cut — prose, wiring and restoration notes in
// the base-effect parking note (internal editorial docs); initBase
// stays exported from inflation-widgets.ts and wild.base stays in the payload.
// Same pass: one averaging table instead of two, index promoted to its own
// h2, rank-turnover paragraph cut, refs renumbered contiguously (1-9), and
// the build script now guards the prose's static claims (silver on top,
// edu ≥ 2x headline).
// Fresh-eyes pass (2026-08-05): subtext rewritten to match the descent (it
// still described the climb), figcaptions trimmed to encoding + source only
// (anything the prose or a widget's live caption already says is gone), and
// the core-inflation aside moved to close "The twelve divisions" so the fold
// rule sits flush against the division indices. Same pass, later: the last
// split's construction paragraph moved below the collectors and the two
// averages, so the tomato's index is built only after the averaging choice
// is on the table (the tell-then-rewind tease it replaced was a climb relic).
//
// Registered in reads-index (kind: 'read') and dispatch No. 2's lead item —
// it ships in the racks, the feed, the sitemap and the index: the draft
// resolved 2026-08-08 and the noindex window closed with it. Data still comes inline
// from public/data/lab/inflation-read.json (scripts/build-read-inflation.mjs)
// — outside the manifest until the read ships, when it moves to the hashed
// /data/economy/ pipeline; its shelf/dispatch cover is a data-drawn line
// (lib/reads/motif.ts) until build-read-thumbs.mjs has a real capture to run.
import StoryPage from '../../../layouts/StoryPage.astro';
import ReaderControls from '../../../components/chart/ReaderControls.astro';
import ScrollProgress from '../../../components/read/ScrollProgress.astro';
import ShareButtons from '../../../components/editorial/ShareButtons.astro';
import { readingMinutes } from '../../../lib/reading-time';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

const data = JSON.parse(readFileSync(fileURLToPath(new URL('../../../../public/data/lab/inflation-read.json', import.meta.url)), 'utf8'));
const payload = JSON.stringify(data);

const MONTHS_FULL = ['January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June', 'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December'];
const mFull = (m: string) => `${MONTHS_FULL[+m.slice(5) - 1]} ${m.slice(0, 4)}`;

const last = data.gap.months[data.gap.months.length - 1];
const month = mFull(last.m);
const headline = last.headline.toFixed(2);
const edu = last.edu.toFixed(2);

const food = data.basket.find((b: any) => /^Food/.test(b.name));
const food2012 = food.combined.w2012.toFixed(2);
const food2024 = food.combined.w2024.toFixed(2);
const ruralFood = food.rural.w2024.toFixed(2);
const urbanFood = food.urban.w2024.toFixed(2);
const housing = data.basket.find((b: any) => /^Housing/.test(b.name));
const housingW = housing.combined.w2024.toFixed(2);
const eduW = data.basket.find((b: any) => /^Education/.test(b.name)).combined.w2024.toFixed(2);

// Part II interpolations — every load-bearing number in the prose comes from
// the generated JSON, so the monthly refresh re-dates the whole read.
// The index itself: the published level, its year-ago twin, the ratio between
// them (which IS the headline), and the retired ruler's last reading.
const lv = data.level;
const lvIdx = lv.idx.toFixed(2);
const lvAgo = lv.idxAgo.toFixed(2);
const lvAgoM = mFull(lv.agoM);
const lvRatio = (lv.idx / lv.idxAgo).toFixed(4);
// the linking factor left the prose in the Aug 2026 tightening pass and
// returned with the descent's long-run figure: the multiplier is now the
// one-line explainer for how the merged index line is drawn
const onOldRuler = (lv.idx / data.verify.linking.combined.official).toFixed(0);
const linkF = data.verify.linking.combined.official.toFixed(4);
// The reconstruction check (data.verify.recon — the twelve published division
// indices reweighted against the published headline, residual under a
// thousandth of a point) left the summit with the July 2026 pass. The figures
// are still in the payload if the claim is ever wanted back.
// The Delhi/Kerala chained-drift figure (data.mixer) left the page with the
// climb restructure — the AM/GM case now lives in the planned collapsible, and
// the mixer series is reserved for a later read.

const tom = data.pyramid.path;
// the same strand read as index levels, which is what actually merges: the
// percentages are only what these come to against their own value a year back
const tomIdx = tom[0].idx.toFixed(2);
const subIdx = tom[1].idx.toFixed(2);
const classIdx = tom[2].idx.toFixed(2);
const groupIdx = tom[3].idx.toFixed(2);
const divIdx = tom[4].idx.toFixed(2);
const wRural = data.pyramid.sectors.rural.toFixed(2);
const wUrban = data.pyramid.sectors.urban.toFixed(2);
const tomInfl = tom[0].infl.toFixed(2);
// the per-floor RATES (tom[1..4].infl) left the prose when the percentage walk
// and the index walk merged into one paragraph: the descent is told in index
// levels now, and the rates would only have said the same thing twice
const tomPaise = Math.round(tom[0].w * 100);
const nItems = data.pyramid.levels.find((l: any) => l.key === 'item').n;
const tomDelta = ((tom[0].w * tom[0].infl) / 100).toFixed(2);

// Part III — every figure below is read straight out of the generated data,
// so the prose re-dates itself with the monthly refresh. (The `veg` block that
// fed the parked base-effect section is gone with it; see the parked doc.)
const wd = data.wild;
// what the average hides: the headline rebuilt from the 358 item weights, and
// rebuilt again without the two jewellery items. The build script refuses to
// emit unless the full rebuild reproduces the published number, so `rebuilt`
// and `published` agreeing below is a check the reader can be told about.
const ct = wd.contrib;
const ctItem = (needle: string) => ct.items.find((i: any) => i.name.startsWith(needle));
const silver = ctItem('Silver');
const petrol = ctItem('Petrol');
const ctHead = ct.published.toFixed(2);
const ctEx = ct.ex.toFixed(2);
const ctDrop = (ct.published - ct.ex).toFixed(2);
const ctJewelW = ct.jewelW.toFixed(2);
const silverX = (petrol.w / silver.w).toFixed(0);
const silverFrom = ct.silver.first.toFixed(0);
const silverFromM = mFull(ct.silver.months[0]);
const silverPeak = ct.silver.peak.toFixed(0);

// many Indias: the p10-p90 band of state rates, month by month since 2016
const st = wd.states;
const stAt = (name: string) => st.latest.rows.find((r: any) => r.name === name);
const stAI = st.latest.ai.toFixed(2);
const stDelhi = stAt('Delhi').v.toFixed(2);
const stYears = st.yearSpread.filter((y: any) => y.y >= '2016' && y.y <= '2025');
const stNarrowest = Math.min(...stYears.map((y: any) => y.avg)).toFixed(1);
const stWidest = Math.max(...stYears.map((y: any) => y.avg)).toFixed(1);
// the rank-turnover paragraph (topHolders / Kerala's reign) was cut in the
// Aug 2026 pass — it contradicted itself (a 12-month reign vs 8 top months of
// 122) and leaned on thin-sample Manipur; the data stays in the payload
const stFirstY = st.firstM.slice(0, 4);
const repoNow = wd.repo.steps[wd.repo.steps.length - 1].r.toFixed(2);

const title = 'Inflation: The Price of Nearly Everything';
const deck = 'One number claims to describe a billion baskets. This is how it is made.';
const subtext = `In three parts: the gap between the headline number and your life; the headline taken apart, floor by floor, down to a collector's tablet; and the number let out into the country.`;
---

<StoryPage title={title} description={deck} og_type="article" og_image="/og/reads/price-of-nearly-everything.png">
  <ScrollProgress />
  <article class="read">
    <nav class="folio" aria-label="Section">
      <a href="/economy">Economy</a><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><a href="/economy/read">Read</a>
    </nav>
    <header class="head">
      <p class="kicker">How it works &middot; Prices &middot; {readingMinutes('price-of-nearly-everything')} min read &middot; data through {month}</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="deck">{deck}</p>
      <p class="subtext">{subtext}</p>
      <div class="toolbar"><ReaderControls /><ShareButtons title={title} variant="icon" /></div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">

      {/* the opening bookend belongs to the whole read, so it sits above the
          first part-mark rather than inside Part I */}
      <figure class="hlfig">
        <div class="hl-area"></div>
        {/* one line: the drawing states the number itself and the opening para
            repeats it; what only the caption can say is the source and the
            splice (this line joins the 2012 and 2024 series with no seam) */}
        <figcaption>Retail inflation since 2014. MoSPI, Consumer Price Index; 2012 series
          to 2025, 2024 series from 2026.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="partmark partmark-first">Part I &middot; The number and you</p>

      <p>On 13 July 2026 the Ministry of Statistics published the number it publishes every
        month: retail inflation. Prices in India, taken together, were
        <strong class="num e e-head">{headline}%</strong> higher than they had been a year
        earlier.<sup class="c"><a href="#r1">1</a></sup> The evening bulletins read it out, the
        papers filed it beside the month before, and by morning it had settled into the corner of
        the page where numbers go to be believed.</p>

      <p>If you were a parent in Delhi that week, holding a school-fee circular, belief may have
        come harder. By the government&rsquo;s own count, the cost of education in the capital was
        running <strong class="num e e-you">{edu}%</strong> above last year, more than double the all-India
        pace,<sup class="c"><a href="#r2">2</a></sup> and the fight over fee hikes had already
        produced a new law and a High Court
        order.<sup class="c"><a href="#r4">4</a></sup><sup class="c"><a href="#r5">5</a></sup>
        None of that turmoil is visible in the {headline} percent.</p>

      <figure class="gapfig widgetfig">
        <div class="gap-area"></div>
        <figcaption>Education in Delhi against the all-India average, year on year; the shaded
          column is the gap, at least double in every month so far. MoSPI, Consumer Price
          Index.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Both numbers are honest. <span class="e e-you">The school bill</span> answers a question
        about one kind of spending in one city. The number on the news answers a stranger
        question: what happened to the price of nearly everything, everywhere, averaged across
        everyone. Economists call it <span class="e e-head">headline inflation</span>, because it
        is the one that makes the headlines. The rest of this piece is
        about how that answer gets made, and how it can land so far from the one your own life
        gives, in either direction, without anyone lying to you. It starts where the number
        itself starts, with a shopping basket.</p>

      <h2>The basket</h2>

      <p>Ask the ministry what inflation measures and the answer is a basket: 358 kinds of things
        Indian households buy, from rice to bus fares to a streaming subscription.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup>
        Each item carries a weight, its share of the average household&rsquo;s spending, taken from
        a national survey of what households actually consume. Food and beverages carry
        <strong class="num">&#8377;{food2024}</strong> of every &#8377;100; housing, water and fuel
        carry &#8377;{housingW}; everything else divides what remains. Multiply each price change
        by its weight, add it all up, and the sum, near enough, is the headline number.</p>

      <p>Strictly there is not one basket but two. <span class="e e-rural">Rural</span> and
        <span class="e e-urban">urban</span> India are surveyed separately, and they spend
        differently: food is &#8377;{ruralFood} of the <span class="e e-rural">rural</span>
        hundred and &#8377;{urbanFood} of the <span class="e e-urban">urban</span> one. The
        number on the news is a blend of both.</p>

      <figure class="rufig widgetfig">
        <div class="ru-area"></div>
        {/* the widget's caption reads the selected division's two rupee
            figures and invites the tap; this one carries the encoding and
            the source */}
        <figcaption>Each division&rsquo;s share of the <span class="e e-rural">rural</span>
          &#8377;100 against its share of the <span class="e e-urban">urban</span> one, 2024
          basket, sorted by the gap between the two. MoSPI, HCES weights.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The important thing about the official basket is that no household actually divides its
        hundred this way. Below is the real basket with its real weights
        and each division&rsquo;s latest rate of inflation. The sliders are yours, and they hold
        one shared &#8377;100. Set the mix to the way your own month actually goes, and watch
        <span class="e e-you">your number</span> walk away from
        <span class="e e-head">the headline</span>.</p>

      <figure class="ybfig widgetfig">
        <div class="yb-area"></div>
        <figcaption>The official basket, yours to reweight; the line traces your number against
          the headline. MoSPI, {month}.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Whatever you set, you are still pricing today&rsquo;s spending habits. Habits drift, and
        every decade or so the basket is rebuilt from scratch to keep up. The latest rebuild
        arrived with the January 2026 bulletin, after thirteen years on the old basket: a fresh
        survey, fresh weights, fresh categories, and a fresh year to count from, with prices now
        measured against 2024 instead of 2012. The list of what was thrown out is a country
        cleaning out a drawer.</p>

      <h2>The portrait</h2>

      <p>Out went the video cassette recorder, the audio cassette, the tape recorder, the radio
        set, second-hand clothing, coir rope. In came streaming subscriptions, pen drives, piped
        cooking gas, the babysitter, the home exercise equipment, and, measured for the first
        time, the rent a rural household pays.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup> The 299 items of the 2012 basket
        became 358, sorted for the first time into the twelve divisions of COICOP, the United
        Nations classification most of the world&rsquo;s statistical agencies use, so
        India&rsquo;s inflation is now stated in the same categories as nearly everyone
        else&rsquo;s. A basket is a measuring instrument, but the two lists together are also a
        portrait: a decade of Indian life, recorded in what a statistician decided was worth
        pricing.</p>

      <p>The weights moved with the habits, and one move matters more than all the others. Food&rsquo;s
        share of the average wallet fell from &#8377;{food2012} to &#8377;{food2024} of every
        &#8377;100, the largest fall in the basket.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup>
        It is the usual signature of a country getting richer: as households earn more, food
        takes a smaller share of what they spend.</p>

      <figure class="bkfig widgetfig">
        <div class="bk-area"></div>
        {/* the widget's own caption reads the selected line ("Of every ₹100
            the average household spends…") and invites the tap, so this one
            carries only what that caption never says: the vintages and the
            like-for-like basis */}
        <figcaption>The 2012 and 2024 CPI baskets, both restated on the 2024 classification
          (MoSPI, HCES weights).</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>None of this rewrites a price anyone paid. The part of a rebuild that genuinely
        changes the number is the weights, the same dials the basket figure above put in your
        hands.</p>

      <p>A basket, in the end, is a shopping list, and the headline is what the list turns into
        once every item on it has been priced and weighed. So far the number has been taken at
        its word. Part II takes it apart: starting at the {headline} percent itself, it opens
        each number to find the numbers it was made from, floor by floor, down to a tomato on
        one market counter.</p>

      <p class="partmark">Part II &middot; How the number is made</p>

      <h2>The index and the headline</h2>

      {/* the descent opens where the climb used to end: the last step of
          arithmetic first, so the reader holds the whole trick (index over
          index) before any machinery arrives. Colour code, held through the
          whole Part: the HEADLINE RATE is saffron, the INDEX it is read
          from is ink. */}
      <p>The <span class="e e-head">{headline} percent</span> makes one claim: prices in
        {month} were {headline} percent higher than in the same month a year earlier. But
        that number is derived, not built. What the ministry builds, and its monthly
        bulletin carries, is an index:
        <strong class="num">{lvIdx}</strong>, which says that the basket of Part I,
        the one that cost &#8377;100 in 2024, cost &#8377;{lvIdx} in {month}. That is all an index
        is: the basket&rsquo;s cost, written as a percentage of what it cost in the base year,
        2024.</p>

      <p>The bulletin also carries the same index for {lvAgoM}:
        <strong class="num">{lvAgo}</strong>. Divide the newer by the older and the answer
        is &times;{lvRatio}, which the news reads out
        as <span class="e e-head">{headline} percent</span>. That is the whole of the last
        step: two published numbers and one division, a month set against the same month a
        year before. Holding the two months a year apart is what makes the seasons cancel:
        December is set against December, harvest against harvest, so a winter spike in
        onions reads as winter rather than inflation.</p>

      <figure class="rlfig ixfig widgetfig">
        <div class="rl-area"></div>
        {/* the live caption owns the scrubbed month's arithmetic; this one
            carries the encoding and the source */}
        <figcaption>The index on top, the headline it produces beneath; scrub a month and
          the mark scrubs twelve behind it. MoSPI, 2024-base series, all-India
          combined.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The base year is a choice, and until the rebuild the choice was 2012. That retired
        series, the one this basket replaced, ran alongside the new one through the whole of
        2025, and the two rulers are joined by a single published number: divide an index
        on the new scale by the linking factor, <span class="nl e-splice">{linkF}</span>, and
        it restates itself on the old one.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup>
        {month}&rsquo;s <strong class="num">{lvIdx}</strong> sits near
        <strong class="num">{onOldRuler}</strong> there, the plainest way of saying that
        prices have roughly doubled since 2012. The figure below runs the whole line back on
        that older ruler, the news&rsquo;s rate riding beneath it.</p>

      <figure class="lrfig ixfig widgetfig">
        <div class="lr-area"></div>
        {/* index row: the retired series continued past the rebuild by the
            linking factor, seam drawn rather than hidden; rate row: the one
            splice the methodology permits (headline only, never categories —
            see oneLine in the generator) */}
        <figcaption>The long run: the index restated on the retired 2012 ruler, the spliced
          headline beneath. The red seam is the 2024 rebuild. The break in the rate is the
          spring of 2020, when the COVID lockdown stopped collection: the ministry imputed
          the two index months but published no rate off an imputed number. MoSPI, all-India
          combined.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>And the <strong class="num">{lvIdx}</strong> is already a blend.
        <span class="e e-rural">Rural</span> and
        <span class="e e-urban">urban</span> India carry the two separate baskets of Part I,
        each priced across its own markets and each with an index of its own, and the published
        number folds the two together by their share of the country&rsquo;s household spending:
        of every &#8377;100 spent nationally, rural households spend
        <span class="nl e-rural">&#8377;{wRural}</span> and urban households
        <span class="nl e-urban">&#8377;{wUrban}</span>. States fold in the same way, each with
        its own baskets, by its share of the national wallet. Every number in this Part is that
        kind of fold. The rest of it keeps opening them, floor by floor, until the numbers stop
        having numbers inside them.</p>

      <figure class="sxfig widgetfig">
        <div class="sx-area"></div>
        {/* the live caption does the blend arithmetic for the tapped month */}
        <figcaption>The rural and urban indices, every month of the 2024 series, with the
          published blend between them as the dashed ink line. MoSPI.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The twelve divisions</h2>

      <p>One floor down, the index splits into twelve. The
        <span class="e e-lvl-div">divisions</span> of Part I, food and beverages, housing,
        transport and the rest, each carry an index of their own, built the same way and
        published every month beside the headline; in {month} the food and beverages division
        stood at <span class="nl e-lvl-div">{divIdx}</span>. Set any of them against its own
        year-ago self and you get that aisle&rsquo;s inflation.</p>

      <figure class="aifig widgetfig">
        <div class="ai-area"></div>
        {/* three tabs, not twelve, and not a dropdown: the section narrates,
            the explore board enumerates, and the funnel below reaches every
            cell */}
        <figcaption>One division&rsquo;s index at a time, 2024-base series; the scrubbed
          month&rsquo;s mark sits on the same line, twelve months behind. MoSPI, all-India
          combined.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>How do twelve indices become one? Not by treating them as equals. Each division
        counts in proportion to its share of the household &#8377;100, and the shares are
        not read off any price: they come from the Household Consumption Expenditure Survey
        of 2023&ndash;24, which asked 2,61,953 households what they actually
        spend.<sup class="c"><a href="#r6">6</a></sup> Their answers are the &#8377;100 of
        Part I, and the rural and urban shares that blended the two indices at the top of
        the descent. The rule is one line: each division&rsquo;s index times its share,
        summed.</p>

      <p>A weighted average behaves like a beam balancing. Each division sits at its own rate
        of price rise, heavy or light according to its share, and the headline is the point
        where the beam balances. Drag any division along the beam and the fulcrum chases it,
        by exactly its weight&rsquo;s worth. The button underneath is subtler: it picks one
        vegetable inside food, the tomato, and doubles its pace, and how far the fulcrum moves
        then is what a half-rupee share can do.</p>

      <figure class="bmfig widgetfig">
        <div class="bm-area"></div>
        {/* "Drag a disc, or tap it for its numbers" is the widget caption's
            opening line — stating it here too was the same sentence twice,
            one line apart */}
        <figcaption>Twelve discs, placed by rate, sized by share. MoSPI, {month}, all-India
          combined.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The shares stay frozen until the next rebuild, and that is a known, accepted
        distortion: households drift away from whatever gets dear, so an aging basket slightly
        overstates the cost of living, which is why the ministry now intends to rebuild every
        few years instead of once in thirteen.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup></p>

      <p>This floor is also where one more famous number is made by subtraction. Set aside the
        aisles that swing with harvests and crude, food and fuel, and the inflation of what
        remains is <span class="e e-core">core inflation</span>, the reading economists watch
        for the trend underneath the weather.</p>

      <figure class="cofig widgetfig">
        <div class="co-area"></div>
        <figcaption>Headline against core on the retired 2012 series, the one long enough to
          show the shape; the buttons add food or fuel back in. MoSPI; ends December 2025,
          where the old series does.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The weighted descent</h2>

      <p>The split does not stop at twelve. <span class="e e-lvl-div">Divisions</span> open
        into <span class="e e-lvl-group">groups</span>, groups into
        <span class="e e-lvl-class">classes</span>, classes into
        <span class="e e-lvl-sub">sub-classes</span>, and sub-classes into the {nItems}
        <span class="e e-lvl-item">items</span> of the basket: four more floors, and the same
        rule binding every one of them to the floor above, each member&rsquo;s index times its
        share, summed. Nothing else happens between a market price and the headline; the rule
        just runs five times. The figure holds the whole tree. Follow the strand already lit,
        from the headline down to the tomato, or tap any cell and follow your own.</p>

      <figure class="pyfig widgetfig">
        <div class="py-area"></div>
        {/* cell-width = share of the ₹100 lives in the widget's own caption
            (bolded, per stage), so it is not restated here */}
        <figcaption>The whole tree, {month}: one number splitting into {nItems} items. Tap any
          cell to follow its strand. MoSPI 2024 weighing diagram, all-India
          combined.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Read down the tomato&rsquo;s strand and the indices tell it floor by floor: the food
        and beverages <span class="e e-lvl-div">division</span> at
        <span class="nl e-lvl-div">{divIdx}</span>; the food
        <span class="e e-lvl-group">group</span> at
        <span class="nl e-lvl-group">{groupIdx}</span>; the vegetables
        <span class="e e-lvl-class">class</span> at
        <span class="nl e-lvl-class">{classIdx}</span>, still under its base-year hundred,
        because vegetables cost less than they did in 2024; the
        <span class="e e-lvl-sub">sub-class</span> at
        <span class="nl e-lvl-sub">{subIdx}</span>; and at the bottom the tomato itself at
        <span class="nl e-lvl-item">{tomIdx}</span>. Each floor is the ones below it, weighed
        and added, and the percentages the bulletin quotes are only what these indices come to
        when set beside their own value a year earlier.</p>

      <p>The strand also prices the tomato&rsquo;s vote in the headline. In {month} the tomato
        stood <span class="nl e-lvl-item">{tomInfl}</span> percent dearer than a year before, and
        its weight is {tomPaise} paise of the &#8377;100. A share does the same work on a
        change as it does on an index: move times share, roughly
        <span class="nl e-lvl-item">{tomDelta}</span> of the
        <span class="e e-head">{headline}</span> points the news read out, from the tomato
        alone.</p>

      <h2>The last split</h2>

      <p>At the bottom the machinery changes, because it has to. The tomato has an index, but
        it has no members: there is no floor below it, only actual tomatoes with actual
        prices, in market after market across the country. Its index has to be made from the
        prices themselves, which makes it a different kind of number, what statisticians call
        an elementary index: the floor the whole tree stands on. Building one means seeing
        the prices first: where they come from.</p>

      <h2>The collectors</h2>

      {/* 1,395, not the annexure's 1,407 urban total: twelve of those are
          online markets, priced without a visit, and this sentence is about
          the walking. The twelve get their own clause in the no-walk
          paragraph below. */}
      {/* "June 2026" is deliberately NOT {month}: the payload carries no
          response-rate field, so a variable month would silently claim 100
          percent for bulletins nobody has checked. Re-pin, or wire a field
          into the generator, when a newer rate is worth quoting. */}
      <p>They come from a walk. Every month, field staff of the National Sample Survey walk
        into 1,465 villages and 1,395 urban markets spread across 434 towns and price the
        basket item by item, on a weekly roster, by personal
        visit.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup> The paper schedules are gone; since the
        2024 series the prices go into a tablet that checks them as they are keyed. In June
        2026 the collection&rsquo;s response rate was 100 percent, in both rural and urban
        India.</p>

      <p>Not every price requires a walk: streaming subscriptions and airfares are read off the
        providers&rsquo; own pages, twelve of the urban markets are online ones priced the same
        way, fuel prices arrive from the petroleum ministry, and rent has
        a standing panel of 19,039 dwellings.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup> Somewhere
        in what the tablets bring back is the tomato, priced in market after market across the
        country.</p>

      <h2>The two averages</h2>

      {/* what gets averaged is each counter's CHANGE, never the rupee
          amounts themselves. The parable below stays deliberately unanchored
          (month moves only); the real anchor, each market's own 2024 price,
          arrives in the construction paragraph that closes the section */}
      <p>The tomato comes back as hundreds of prices, and so does every other item; what never
        comes back is how much anyone bought at which counter, so nothing says which counter
        should matter most. Each
        counter&rsquo;s price change has to collapse into a single figure for the item, and
        there are two ways to average changes: the familiar way, add and divide; and the
        ministry&rsquo;s way, multiply and take the root.</p>

      <p>The familiar average has a weakness, and it takes only two months to show. Prices
        bounce: a counter doubles in a bad month and comes back in the good one that follows,
        and the counters never bounce quite in step. The table follows two of them through one
        swap.</p>

      <figure class="swfig">
        <table class="sw-table">
          <thead><tr><th>two counters, &#8377;20 each</th><th>the month&rsquo;s moves</th><th>multiplied average</th><th>added average</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>month one</td><td>&#8377;40 (&times;2) and &#8377;10 (&times;0.5)</td><td class="sw-mult">&times;1</td><td>&times;1.25</td></tr>
            <tr><td>month two</td><td>&#8377;20 (&times;0.5) and &#8377;20 (&times;2)</td><td class="sw-mult">&times;1</td><td>&times;1.25</td></tr>
            <tr><td>the whole trip</td><td>back to &#8377;20</td><td class="sw-mult">&times;1</td><td class="sw-hot">&times;1.56</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>A doubling that reverses; &times;2 means the price doubled that month, and
          the months multiply into the whole trip.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Every price ends back at &#8377;20, so the honest answer for the whole trip is that
        nothing happened. Each month&rsquo;s moves are &times;2 and &times;0.5. Multiply them
        and take the root: &times;1, both months, and the trip is &times;1. Add and divide:
        &times;1.25, both months, and the overshoots multiply into &times;1.56, a little
        inflation that never happened, permanently on the record.</p>

      <p>That is why, in each state, an item&rsquo;s single figure is the multiplied average
        of its market quotes: the changes multiplied together and the root
        taken.<sup class="c"><a href="#r3">3</a></sup></p>

      {/* TODO(collapsible) — "Why multiply? The averaging mistake that cost Britain billions".
          Planned fold, in order: the two averages side by side with a drag-two-numbers
          interactive (the AM/GM gap closes only when the numbers meet); the shopper-substitution
          rationale (cut from the main text Aug 2026 — it asserted the GM-tracks-shoppers link
          without the property that earns it); the semicircle proof
          (rebuild initProof from git history); and Britain in one paragraph — RPI's added
          average, the ~0.9pp/yr formula effect, the pensions, student loans and rail fares it
          governed, the 2013 loss of National Statistic status. (The ₹156 ledger and the
          two-routes table from earlier plans are covered by the two main-text tables now.)
          The Delhi/Kerala drift figure (initDrift + data.mixer) is reserved for a
          later read. The cut UK RPI sources return with this fold
          (unnumbered — since the Aug 2026 renumbering, r7 is the RBI repo ref). */}

      {/* deliberately elided (Aug 2026, flagged and accepted): the GM is
          really per state x sector, and the strand's combined item index is
          those folds blended — the recap's "two trees blended into one" and
          the sentence added to ref r3 carry the sector story, and spelling
          the folds out here was load at the exact moment of the payoff.
          Don't "fix" this back in. */}
      <p>The tomato&rsquo;s index can now be built end to end. Every price the ministry
        holds for it is one market&rsquo;s quote, and each is first read against what the
        tomato cost in that same market in 2024: not a rupee amount but a ratio, a
        &#8377;30 tomato that cost &#8377;20 back then reading as &times;1.5. Multiply every
        market&rsquo;s ratio together, take the root, put the answer on a scale of a hundred,
        and the result is the
        <span class="e e-lvl-item">item</span>&rsquo;s index for the month, the
        <span class="nl e-lvl-item">{tomIdx}</span> of the strand.</p>

      <p>That is the floor of the tree, and the whole of the machine. Ratios averaged into an
        item&rsquo;s index; indices weighed and summed, floor over floor, five times; two trees
        blended into one; the result divided by its own year-ago self and read out on the
        news. The descent ends where the number begins, at a counter where somebody sells
        tomatoes. Everything above it is the same move repeated: many numbers folded into
        one.</p>

      <p class="partmark">Part III &middot; The number and the country</p>

      <p>Everything so far has been about how the number is built. What follows is about what
        the averaging folds away, and about the one thing that makes the folding matter: this
        average is not only published. It is obeyed.</p>

      {/* PARKED (2026-08-01): the base-effect section ("How to read the chart" — the
          rate/index/mark hues, the vegetables July-2024 case, the two-strip scrubber)
          moved out whole. Prose, wiring and restoration notes:
          the base-effect parking note in the internal editorial docs.
          Undecided: its own read, a collapsible near "The index and the headline",
          or a full cut. initBase stays in inflation-widgets.ts; wild.base stays
          in the payload. */}

      <h2>What the average hides</h2>

      <p>Part II opened a single number into a tree of weights. Weights are easy
        to nod at and hard to feel, so here is what one does when the price behind it moves. In
        {month} the item that pushed the headline hardest was none of the usual heavyweights.
        It was <span class="nl e-lvl-item">silver jewellery</span>, which carries
        &#8377;{silver.w.toFixed(2)} of the hundred and rose
        <span class="nl e-lvl-item">{silver.yoy.toFixed(0)}</span> percent on the year. Weight
        times rise comes to {silver.c.toFixed(3)} of a percentage point, which is more than
        petrol contributed, and petrol carries {silverX} times the weight.</p>

      <p>The metal did something unusual to get there. The silver jewellery index stood at
        {silverFrom} in {silverFromM} and has since touched {silverPeak}: it nearly tripled
        inside two years, which is not a thing food or rent can do. Weight decides how loudly
        an item can speak; the price decides whether it shouts.</p>

      <figure class="ctfig widgetfig">
        <div class="ct-area"></div>
        {/* the widget's caption already opens "Each bar is one item's
            contribution: its own rate times its share of the ₹100", and the
            re-run-not-subtracted method note lives in the next paragraph and
            ref 8 — this one keeps only the scope */}
        <figcaption>The twelve largest contributions of {nItems} items, {month}. MoSPI item
          indices; weights from the 2024 weighing diagram.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The button under the figure is worth dwelling on. Gold and silver jewellery together are
        &#8377;{ctJewelW} of the hundred, less than a rupee. Rebuild the index over the other
        356 items, using the same arithmetic the ministry uses, and {month}&rsquo;s headline
        falls from <span class="e e-head">{ctHead}</span> percent to
        <span class="e e-head">{ctEx}</span>.<sup class="c"><a href="#r8">8</a></sup> That is {ctDrop} points of a national inflation
        rate coming from two items most households did not buy this year.</p>

      <p>None of this is an error. The weights are published, the item indices are published, and
        the rebuild above reproduces the ministry&rsquo;s own headline to the second decimal
        before it removes anything. That is the point. The average is working exactly as
        designed, and the design lets a sliver of the basket carry a visible share of the
        result.</p>

      <h2>Many Indias</h2>

      <p>The other thing the average folds away is the country. India&rsquo;s inflation is
        published as one rate, and there has never been a month in which one rate was close to
        true. In {month} the {st.latest.n} states and union territories ran from
        {st.latest.lo.toFixed(2)} percent to {st.latest.hi.toFixed(2)}, with the All-India
        number at <span class="e e-head">{stAI}</span> and Delhi at {stDelhi}.</p>

      <figure class="sdfig widgetfig">
        <div class="sd-area"></div>
        {/* the widget's caption counts and dates them ("36 states and union
            territories, {month}…"), so the count and month stay out of this
            one */}
        <figcaption>One dot per state, stacked where they crowd, with
          <span class="e e-head">All India</span> as the line through them. Tap a dot for its
          name. MoSPI, 2024 series, combined.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>This is not one bad month. Ignore the extremes at both ends, which are mostly small
        union territories with thin samples, and the middle eighty percent of states was between
        {stNarrowest} and {stWidest} points wide in every year of the old basket, {stFirstY} to
        2025.<sup class="c"><a href="#r9">9</a></sup> The gap has never closed.</p>

      <h2>The number answers back</h2>

      <p>Most numbers describe the country. This one is also asked to steer it. Since 2016 the
        Reserve Bank&rsquo;s Monetary Policy Committee has been bound by law to keep the number
        this piece has been building at 4 percent, give or take two, and its instrument is
        <span class="e e-repo">the repo rate</span>: what banks pay to borrow overnight from the
        Reserve Bank, and so the floor under every other rate in the
        country.<sup class="c"><a href="#r7">7</a></sup></p>

      <p>The mechanism is blunt and slow: raise it and borrowing gets dearer, households and
        firms spend less, demand cools, and prices rise more slowly, most of it arriving about
        a year later. That lag is why <span class="e e-repo">the staircase</span> below always
        looks late against the line; it has held at {repoNow} percent since the June 2026
        meeting.</p>

      <figure class="rofig widgetfig">
        <div class="ro-area"></div>
        <figcaption><span class="e e-repo">The policy repo rate</span> as a staircase over
          <span class="e e-head">headline inflation</span>, 2014 to {month}, the mandate drawn
          as its three numbers. CPI spliced across the 2024 rebuild; repo steps from RBI
          announcements.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The limit matters more than the mechanism. Dearer money works on demand, not supply. It
        cannot make it rain on a failed crop, and it cannot move the world price of a metal. The
        latest number in front of the committee is {ctHead} percent, of which {ctDrop} points is
        jewellery. Worse, the usual defence against exactly this problem does not catch it: core
        inflation removes only food and fuel, which leaves gold and silver sitting inside
        it.</p>

      <figure class="scfig">
        <div class="sc-area"></div>
        <figcaption>The line the read opened on, drawn to its end, and the end opened: one
          number unfolding back into <span class="nl e-lvl-div">12 divisions</span>,
          <span class="nl e-lvl-group">43 groups</span>,
          <span class="nl e-lvl-class">92 classes</span>,
          <span class="nl e-lvl-sub">162 sub-classes</span> and
          <span class="nl e-lvl-item">358 items</span>. Tap to redraw. MoSPI.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Which brings us back to the school-fee circular in Delhi. It was inside the headline the
        whole time. Education carries &#8377;{eduW} of the hundred; Delhi is one state&rsquo;s
        share of that; and what comes out of the merge is averaged with every household that paid
        no fees at all. The parent&rsquo;s year is in the number, and it is outvoted.</p>

      <p>That is not a flaw to repair. An average is a decision about what to fold together, and
        this one is read by a committee that can move the price of money. Next month it will be
        back in the same corner of the page, one number, unchanged. What has changed is that it
        comes apart in the hand. It is the price of nearly everything, and the heavy word in that
        phrase has always been <em>nearly</em>.</p>

      {/* Open items live in the plan doc, not on the page: the Part II averaging
          fold (AM/GM + UK RPI) and the parked base-effect section. */}

      <ShareButtons title={title} />

      <nav class="folio folio-end" aria-label="More in this dispatch">
